Title:
Beyond the APIs: A Windows Internals Deep Dive into Process Injection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains classic Windows process injection by connecting common user-mode APIs to the underlying OS internals (virtual memory, VADs, page tables, handles, and threads).  
- It walks through a working DLL injection PoC using the standard chain: OpenProcess → VirtualAllocEx → WriteProcessMemory → CreateRemoteThread → LoadLibraryA, and clarifies what each step changes inside the target process.  
- The article emphasizes how Windows represents memory (VAS, paging, VAD tree) and how PE sections become mapped image regions with specific protections during LoadLibrary.  
- It also covers the security model behind cross-process operations (access tokens, security descriptors, handle rights) and why missing rights break later injection stages.  
- From a defender perspective, it enumerates observable artifacts at each stage (suspicious handle access, private memory allocations, cross-process writes, remote thread creation, and new module loads).  
- It briefly contrasts this “loud” technique with stealthier variants like APC injection, thread hijacking, process hollowing, and section mapping/manual map.  
- Useful for red teamers/malware developers learning fundamentals and for blue team/EDR engineers mapping telemetry to concrete OS state changes.

Technical Focus:
- Windows virtual memory: VAS, paging, page faults, page tables  
- VAD (Virtual Address Descriptor) tree and memory region types (Private vs Image)  
- PE loading and section-to-memory protection mapping (.text/.rdata/.data/.reloc)  
- Windows object security: handles, access tokens, security descriptors, access rights  
- Remote thread execution and scheduling (CreateRemoteThread, thread start addresses)  
- Detection artifacts/telemetry for injection stages

Use Cases:
- Build or review a baseline DLL injection PoC for lab testing
- Map API-level injection behavior to memory forensics (VADs, module lists, protections)
- Develop EDR detections for cross-process handle access, memory writes, and remote threads
- Train analysts to distinguish Private RW allocations vs Image-backed mappings
- Compare “classic” injection to alternatives (APC, thread hijack, hollowing, section mapping)

Keywords:
process injection, DLL injection, Windows internals, OpenProcess, VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, CreateRemoteThread, LoadLibraryA, virtual address space, VAD, VAD tree, page table, page fault, PE loader, section protections, Image vs Private memory, access token, security descriptor, handle rights, EDR telemetry, module enumeration, NtCreateSection, NtMapViewOfSection, APC injection, thread hijacking, process hollowing, manual mapping